  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest analysis this morning shows a reinforcing\nsurge of cooler and drier air making its way southward across\neastern NC with a 1023mb high centered over the Great Lakes\nridging in from the north. The light winds and relative clear\nskies earlier this morning promoted patchy and shallow but \ndense fog in some spots, but the increasing N winds behind this\nfront and dropping Tds are helping scour this out. Not expecting\nany visibility issues area-wide for the morning commute.\n\nTds will drop further today as high pressure builds further \ninto tomorrow, resulting in very pleasant mild and dry days \nwith highs near climo in the 75-80 range (low 70s OBX), but \ncooler than normal lows well down into the 50s for the mainland,\nand perhaps a few 40s for the normally cooler sheltered \nlocales. The strongest signal for this is on Wednesday morning \nwhen the high crests overhead and environment is most favored \nfor radiational cooling. A few spotty lows in the upper 40s are\npossible Thurs AM as well with light winds although TDs will be\na touch higher in the morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mid-level zonal flow returns Thu into the \nweekend, and this will bring a modest warming trend back to \nENC. SW flow returns by the weekend as low pressure over the\nsouthwestern Gulf meanders and then gradually lifts\nnortheastward towards the MS River Valley. The evolution of this\nlow still has plenty of question marks, especially as it\ninteracts with the stalled frontal boundary, but a general\nconsensus remains that as the low lifts to our west, frontal\nboundary will also lift northward back across the Carolinas with\nbuilding WAA and deepening tropical moisture. Coupled with\nincreasingly meridional mid-level flow, increasing rain chances\nare favored across much of the southeastern CONUS including\neastern NC. Above-average PWATs (not a surprise for advection \nhaving Gulf origins) point to a risk of heavier rainfall with \nprobabilities of 24-hour precipitation exceeding 1\" up to 20-40%\nper some ensembles.",
